from mk import mk
mk("y9eDmBUC8ZU","Physically Modeled MPE Strings in Surge XT",
 "bowed-string-noise-exciter-keytracked-comb-mpe-pressure-timbre-body-combs",
 "Physically modelled bowed string: key-tracked noise exciters into a 100%-wet key-tracked comb, MPE pressure on gain/resonance, MPE timbre fading in a 3rd-harmonic comb, and a three-comb instrument body that retunes cello to bass, viola or violin",
 "Build a playable bowed-string voice from synthesis only (no bow sample): a quiet noise exciter feeds a positive comb filter with full key tracking, maximum resonance, 100% wet and cutoff at C4 so it plays in the right octave. MPE pressure (through a shaped response curve) raises exciter gain and lifts comb resonance from about 80 for a scratchy light touch. MPE timbre crossfades in a second key-tracked comb at G5 (third harmonic, resonance ~40%) only near the top of the Y range. A fixed three-comb body (centre a little below 100 Hz, offsets of almost an octave and nearly three octaves, feedback ~15%, tone to taste, pans centred) sets the instrument size. Tame exciter bleed by keeping noise strongest near the played pitch: two S&H-noise oscillators, one optimised for the high end (low cut, key track +10) and one for the low end (high cut, key track −10). Delta beyond our existing noise→resonator and Karplus-style skills: MPE expression mapping plus key-tracked exciter colour and a body comb trio.",
 ["Synth with per-voice comb filters that key-track (Surge XT is free/open source), or Web Audio: per-voice feedback delay comb tuned to note frequency (implementation note)","MPE controller or pressure/Y-axis CC sources","Noise oscillators (white/coloured and S&H/correlated noise)","Post FX comb/resonator block with three combs and a reverb"],
 [("note","midi","Played pitch (drives comb key tracking and exciter colour)",True),("pressure","cc","MPE pressure / channel aftertouch",True),("timbre","cc","MPE Y-axis (CC74)",False),("instrument_size","enum","cello | double bass | viola | violin",False)],
 [("bowed_string_voice","patch","Expressive synthetic bowed string patch"),("body_settings","table","Body comb centre/offset/feedback per instrument size")],
 ["Values are those spoken in the transcript (resonance ~80 base, G5 second comb ~40% resonance, body centre <100 Hz cello / ~50 Hz bass, feedback ~15%, S&H noise −10 with gain −38 then −48, key track ±10). Others are 'to taste'.","Exciter noise always bleeds through somewhat. The presenter suggests a coded physical-modelling synth could subtract the exciter at the end of the chain; that is an idea, not a demonstrated step.","Body combs are global FX and do not track per voice; feedback too high gives metallic drones.","Back up patches before updating Surge (the presenter lost patches on update)."],
 [("Initialise the patch. Route a basic noise oscillator into filter 1 set to comb positive with key tracking full, resonance maximum, 100% wet and cutoff C4. Keep the noise level low and set the overall gain very low before first playing.","comb resonator","A pitched but harsh, robotic string-like tone"),
  ("Add a concert-hall reverb, then set up an MSEG-style response curve driven by MPE pressure (loop off, rate disabled, phase ← pressure ~99%). Use it to raise exciter/output gain and to lift comb resonance from ~80 (unipolar) back toward full.","pressure","Light touch gives a scratchy bowed attack; pressing harder sounds fuller and more resonant"),
  ("Add filter 2 as a second key-tracked comb at G5 (third harmonic of C4), resonance ~40%, filter mode with two filters in series/blend (S2-type), filter balance fully toward filter 1. Make a second curve named 'timbre' driven by MPE Y (≈49% because it reads as bipolar, base 50%) and shape it so the balance only moves toward filter 2 at the top of the Y range; add pressure to its resonance.","timbre harmonic","Sliding up adds a bright third-harmonic overtone; keep subtle, it can shriek"),
  ("Add an instrument body with a three-comb FX block: pans 0, centre a little below 100 Hz (cello), offsets of almost an octave and closer to three octaves, feedback down to ~15%, tone low for a vintage recording sound or higher (or slightly less mix) for 'in the room'. Lower the mix of the upper combs so the centre resonates strongest.","body","Thin buzzy tone gains a woody cello body, not metallic ringing"),
  ("Resize the instrument: move the body centre to ~50 Hz and lower noise colour for double bass; raise centre, noise colour and tone by ear for viola and further for violin. Play in the instrument's own range.","size","Same patch reads as bass, cello, viola or violin"),
  ("Tame noise: noise near the comb cutoff resonates nicely, noise far from it sounds like hiss. Key-track the noise colour upward. Better: mute the basic noise and use two S&H-noise oscillators (level ~−10, exciter gain ~−38 then ~−48), tune correlation/width by ear (width can give a formant-like bow character). Oscillator 1 handles high notes (low cut, key track +10) and oscillator 2 handles low notes (high cut, key track −10).","exciter","Less dark rumble on high notes and enough low end on bass notes"),
  ("Optionally stack a cello-body convolution IR after the combs (watch the low-end build-up and EQ it), then fine-tune levels.","finish","Expressive, playable string with controlled noise")],
 ["The comb cutoff at C4 with full key tracking plays at the correct pitch on a tuner.","Changing pressure audibly changes scratchiness and level; Y-axis adds the third harmonic only near the top.","Moving the body centre from ~100 Hz to ~50 Hz shifts the perceived instrument size down.","Highest and lowest notes have less broadband hiss than with a single un-tracked noise source."],
 ["physical-modelling","bowed-string","comb-filter","mpe","noise-exciter","surge-xt","strings","web-audio"],
 note="Long single-take tutorial; on-screen-only values omitted.")

mk("yuQqY0dx2Rw","Multiphonics CV-3 FM Core Patch Reconstruction with Adam—Resonant Tines",
 "fm-resonant-tines-keys-index-as-cutoff-three-op-stack-macros-mpe-mods",
 "FM electric-keys 'resonant tines' patch built on the subtractive analogy: modulation index as cutoff with its own envelope, a 10:1 bell ratio, a third operator with a no-sustain envelope, three macros (index, decay, ratio spread) and mod-wheel vibrato, aftertouch tremolo and an LFO auto-panner",
 "Design a playable FM keys/tines patch by treating FM depth (modulation index) like a filter cutoff. Start with two sine operators: slow modulator gives vibrato, then speed it up to audio rate to hear sidebands. A 1:1 ratio gives a saw-like full series; 2:1 shifts the spectrum (the presenter compares it to a square) and 10:1 gives the bell/tine. Shape the carrier with a multi-stage envelope (instant attack, short transient decay, sustain-like stage, release) and the modulator depth with its own envelope that starts rich and falls slightly. Add a third modulator operator with a fast-attack, no-sustain envelope, then build macros for index (brightness), decay (all falling-stage times) and modulator ratios (spread wide in opposite directions, a formant-like control). Finish with performance modulation (mod wheel → LFO vibrato from ~3.2 rate, aftertouch → VCA tremolo, a third controller → LFO panner) and a short delay plus bright light long reverb. More specific than generic FM bell skills: macro layout and expressive modulation for a keys patch.",
 ["FM-capable synth or modular (operators with FM input, multi-stage envelopes); Web Audio: OscillatorNode modulators into a carrier's frequency AudioParam via GainNode index (implementation note)","Spectrum analyser for feedback","Controller with mod wheel and aftertouch (or MPE)"],
 [("carrier_ratio","number","Carrier frequency ratio (1 = in tune with the keyboard)",True),("mod_ratios","list","Modulator ratios, e.g. 10:1 for the tine; also try 2 and 10",True),("macros","list","Index, decay, ratio spread",False)],
 [("fm_keys_patch","patch","Polyphonic FM resonant-tine keys patch with three macros"),("mod_map","table","Controller → vibrato / tremolo / pan routing")],
 ["Only the transcript's spoken values are used (8 Hz LFO-rate demo, ratios 1:1, 2:1, 10:1, LFO ~3.2). Envelope times are described shapes, not numbers.","Use carrier envelope to control level directly (no extra VCA needed when the operator has a level/envelope input).","Ratio-spread macro gives unpredictable results by design; cap ranges if it gets harsh."],
 [("Patch one sine operator to the output with a spectrum analyser. Add a second operator at LFO rate (~8 Hz) into the first's FM input: you hear vibrato. Raise it to audio rate and key-track both from the keyboard to hear sidebands.","fm basics","Vibrato becomes sidebands/new harmonics"),
  ("Compare ratios: 1:1 gives a saw-like spectrum with FM depth acting as cutoff; 2:1 changes the harmonic pattern (square-like per the presenter) and the harmonic tail detaches; 10:1 gives a classic FM keys/bell. Make the voice polyphonic.","ratios","Clear timbre changes with ratio; depth behaves like a filter"),
  ("Carrier amplitude: multi-stage envelope used like an ADSR (instant attack to max, short transient decay, a sustain-like third stage, last stage as release), gated by the keyboard.","amp env","Keys-style note shape"),
  ("Modulator depth: give the FM index its own envelope: rich at the start, falling slightly, staying fairly rich.","index env","Bright attack that mellows"),
  ("Add a third operator as an extra modulator with a fast attack and no sustain (falls to zero, a bit longer than the transient). Check the carrier stays at ratio 1 on the analyser while modulator ratios like 2 and 10 enrich the attack.","3rd operator","Spectacular evolving attack harmonics, pitch stays in tune"),
  ("Macros: (1) FM index across all modulators (brightness/'cutoff'), (2) decay: the falling-stage time of all envelopes, (3) modulator ratios moved wide in opposite directions (one up high, one down low) for a formant-like colour.","macros","Three performable timbre controls"),
  ("Performance mods: mod wheel speeds an LFO from ~3.2 and sets its level into all operators' fine-tune (slight detune for tremble/vibrato); aftertouch drives a biased VCA after the carrier for tremolo; a third controller drives an LFO panner. Add a short delay and a bright, light, long reverb.","expression + fx","Expressive tine keys with vibrato, tremolo and pan movement")],
 ["With index at zero only a sine is heard; raising the index adds harmonics, like opening a filter.","The fundamental stays at the played pitch for all modulator ratio settings.","Each macro audibly changes brightness, decay length or formant colour.","Mod wheel adds vibrato, aftertouch adds tremolo, and the panner moves the sound left to right."],
 ["fm-synthesis","fm-keys","bell","tines","macros","modulation-index","mpe","aftertouch"],
 note="Sound-pack patch reconstruction; pack promo dropped and no pack content copied.")
